\section*{Metric Markov cotype two of ell1}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 332; 2026-10-05), the asserted result is that real $\ell_1$ has metric Markov cotype two, giving uniformly controlled Lipschitz extension from Hilbert-space subsets to this target.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 332; 2026-10-05), finite cut representations reconstruct an expected coordinatewise median of three stopped-walk endpoints. A flat-map martingale and quartic potential control the geometric endpoint costs, which a metric comparison converts to the Ces\`aro energy needed for cotype.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The paper asserts metric Markov cotype two for real $\ell_1$ and controlled extension from Hilbert-space subsets. Those are the geometric objects in this question. This catalogue does not establish complete coverage and keeps the entry partial.

\subsection{Formal verification}
No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 1 (family 332; 2026-10-05).

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

